    "candidate_text": "Define integers \\(a_0=1\\), \\(a_1=2\\), and\n\\[\na_{k+2}=4a_{k+1}-a_k-1\\qquad(k\\ge0).\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nF(x,y)=x^2+y^2+x+y-4xy.\n\\]\nWe have \\(F(a_0,a_1)=F(1,2)=0\\). Suppose \\(F(a_k,a_{k+1})=0\\). Regarding\n\\[\nF(a_{k+1},X)=X^2+(1-4a_{k+1})X+a_{k+1}^2+a_{k+1}\n\\]\nas a quadratic in \\(X\\), one root is \\(a_k\\), by the symmetry of \\(F\\). By Vieta's formula, its other root is\n\\[\n4a_{k+1}-1-a_k=a_{k+2}.\n\\]\nHence \\(F(a_{k+1},a_{k+2})=0\\). Inductively,\n\\[\nF(a_k,a_{k+1})=0\n\\]\nfor every \\(k\\ge0\\).\n\nThe sequence consists of positive, strictly increasing integers. Indeed, \\(a_1>a_0\\ge1\\); and if \\(a_{k+1}>a_k\\ge1\\), then, since the terms are integers,\n\\[\na_{k+2}-a_{k+1}=3a_{k+1}-a_k-1\\ge2a_{k+1}>0.\n\\]\nThus all pairs \\((a_k,a_{k+1})\\) are distinct pairs of positive integers.\n\nFinally, \\(F(a_k,a_{k+1})=0\\) gives\n\\[\na_k^2+a_{k+1}^2+a_k+a_{k+1}=4a_ka_{k+1}.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(a_ka_{k+1}>0\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{a_k+1}{a_{k+1}}+\\frac{a_{k+1}+1}{a_k}=4.\n\\]\nTherefore infinitely many pairs of positive integers satisfy the required condition.",
